/// `thing.cml` and `thing.py` both exist: the `.cml` is the module `thing`, and the Python file
/// beside it is not a Camel module at all, whatever it holds. A package's own `thing.py` with the
/// same stem as `thing.cml` used to be compiled as the module `thing`, which reports a syntax
/// error from inside a file the run was never asked to read.
import { value } from thing

func main(): int sync {
    println(value)
    return 0
}